-- title:
--   Averaged hypothesis $\frac{1}{T}\sum_{t=1}^T h_t$
-- statement:
--   Let $h_1, \dots, h_T$ be the hypotheses generated by an on-line algorithm $A$ on a sample $S$ of size $T$, with predictions in a real vector space $E$. The **averaged hypothesis** is the pointwise average
--
--   $$
--   \Big(\frac{1}{T} \sum_{t=1}^T h_t\Big)(x) = \frac{1}{T} \sum_{t=1}^T h_t(x), \qquad x \in X.
--   $$
--
--   This is the hypothesis whose generalization error Theorem 8.15 bounds: it converts the output of an on-line learner into a single batch predictor.
--
--   **Formalization Note** When every $h_t$ takes values in a convex set $Y' \subseteq E$ (as in Theorem 8.15), so does the average.
-- source:
--   Mohri, Rostamizadeh & Talwalkar, Foundations of Machine Learning, 2nd ed., MIT Press 2018, pp. 202–203 (PDF pp. 219–220), §8.4, Theorem 8.15, the hypothesis (1/T) Σ_{t=1}^T h_t

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_MohriFoML_OnlineToBatch_OnlineAlgorithm
import Definitions.Def_MohriFoML_OnlineToBatch_hypSeq

namespace MohriFoML.OnlineToBatch

/-- **Averaged hypothesis `(1/T) ∑_{t=1}^T h_t`.**
Mohri, Rostamizadeh & Talwalkar, Foundations of Machine Learning, 2nd ed., MIT Press 2018,
§8.4, pp. 202–203 (PDF pp. 219–220), Theorem 8.15: the hypothesis `(1/T) ∑_{t=1}^T h_t`.

`avgHyp A S x = (1/T) • ∑_{t} hypSeq A S t x`, the pointwise average of the predictions of the
`T` hypotheses generated by `A` on the sample `S : Fin T → X × Y`.

**Formalization Note.** Predictions live in a real vector space `E`; the book's prediction set
`Y'` is a subset of `E` in the theorems, and is convex there, so the average of predictions in
`Y'` is again in `Y'`. -/
noncomputable def avgHyp {X Y E : Type*} [AddCommGroup E] [Module ℝ E] {T : ℕ}
    (A : OnlineAlgorithm X Y E) (S : Fin T → X × Y) : X → E :=
  fun x => (1 / (T : ℝ)) • ∑ t : Fin T, hypSeq A S t x

end MohriFoML.OnlineToBatch


